NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Svelte table, then shows how to layer on your first feature."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This version of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/svelte-table"},{"type":"text","value":" only supports Svelte 5 or newer. For Svelte 3/4 support, use version 8 of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/svelte-table"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/svelte-table","lang":"bash"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"your-first-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your First Table"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The component below is complete. Paste it into a Svelte 5 app and you will see a working table."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import {\n    createTable,\n    FlexRender,\n    tableFeatures,\n  } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n  import type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\n  // 1. Define the shape of your data\n  type Person = {\n    firstName: string\n    lastName: string\n    age: number\n  }\n\n  // 2. Store data with a $state rune for reactivity\n  let data = $state<Array<Person>>([\n    { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n    { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n    { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n  ])\n\n  // 3. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\n  const features = tableFeatures({})\n\n  // 4. Define your columns\n  const columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n    {\n      accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n      header: 'First Name',\n      cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n    },\n    {\n      accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n      id: 'lastName',\n      header: () => 'Last Name',\n      cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n    },\n    {\n      accessorKey: 'age',\n      header: () => 'Age',\n    },\n  ]\n\n  // 5. Create the table instance\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return data // a getter keeps the table in sync with the $state rune\n    },\n  })\n</script>\n\n<!-- 6. Render markup from the table instance APIs -->\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    {#each table.getHeaderGroups() as headerGroup (headerGroup.id)}\n      <tr>\n        {#each headerGroup.headers as header (header.id)}\n          <th>\n            {#if !header.isPlaceholder}\n              <FlexRender {header} />\n            {/if}\n          </th>\n        {/each}\n      </tr>\n    {/each}\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    {#each table.getRowModel().rows as row (row.id)}\n      <tr>\n        {#each row.getAllCells() as cell (cell.id)}\n          <td>\n            <FlexRender {cell} />\n          </td>\n        {/each}\n      </tr>\n    {/each}\n  </tbody>\n</table>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A few things to note:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({})"},{"type":"text","value":" declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({...})"},{"type":"text","value":" when you need them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Passing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" through a getter ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get data() { return data }"},{"type":"text","value":") lets the table track the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"$state"},{"type":"text","value":" rune, so reassigning "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" updates the table."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" component renders the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footer"},{"type":"text","value":" definitions from your columns. It handles plain values, Svelte components wrapped with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderComponent"},{"type":"text","value":", and snippets wrapped with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderSnippet"},{"type":"text","value":" (see the "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-snippets","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Snippets example"}]},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the full "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-create-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic createTable example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a runnable version with more columns and a footer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a Feature: Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Features are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSortingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":", and wire a click handler into the header markup."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import {\n    createSortedRowModel,\n    createTable,\n    FlexRender,\n    rowSortingFeature,\n    sortFns,\n    tableFeatures,\n  } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\n  const features = tableFeatures({\n    rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n    sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n    sortFns,\n  })\n\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return data\n    },\n  })\n</script>\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    {#each table.getHeaderGroups() as headerGroup (headerGroup.id)}\n      <tr>\n        {#each headerGroup.headers as header (header.id)}\n          <th>\n            {#if !header.isPlaceholder}\n              <button\n                disabled={!header.column.getCanSort()}\n                onclick={header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()}\n              >\n                <FlexRender {header} />\n                {#if header.column.getIsSorted() === 'asc'}\n                  🔼\n                {:else if header.column.getIsSorted() === 'desc'}\n                  🔽\n                {/if}\n              </button>\n            {/if}\n          </th>\n        {/each}\n      </tr>\n    {/each}\n  </thead>\n  <!-- tbody unchanged from above -->\n</table>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature and its row model together in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({...})"},{"type":"text","value":", then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/sorting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to Go Next"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table state."}]},{"type":"text","value":" In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms, and the Svelte adapter installs rune-based reactivity for you. You usually do not need to manage state yourself. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" for starting values and call feature APIs like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.nextPage()"},{"type":"text","value":". When your app should own a state slice, or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/table-state.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It is the foundational guide for everything else."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature guides."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each feature has its own guide, such as "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/column-filtering.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Filtering"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/pagination.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/row-selection.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Selection"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/column-visibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Visibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable tables."}]},{"type":"text","value":" When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { createAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/composable-tables.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable Tables Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Browse the runnable "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-create-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte examples"}]},{"type":"text","value":", from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"your-first-table","text":"Your First Table","level":2},{"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","text":"Add a Feature: Sorting","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to Go Next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Svelte table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> This version of `@tanstack/svelte-table` only supports Svelte 5 or newer. For Svelte 3/4 support, use version 8 of `@tanstack/svelte-table`.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/svelte-table\n```\n\n## Your First Table\n\nThe component below is complete. Paste it into a Svelte 5 app and you will see a working table.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import {\n    createTable,\n    FlexRender,\n    tableFeatures,\n  } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n  import type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\n  // 1. Define the shape of your data\n  type Person = {\n    firstName: string\n    lastName: string\n    age: number\n  }\n\n  // 2. Store data with a $state rune for reactivity\n  let data = $state<Array<Person>>([\n    { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n    { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n    { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n  ])\n\n  // 3. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\n  const features = tableFeatures({})\n\n  // 4. Define your columns\n  const columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n    {\n      accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n      header: 'First Name',\n      cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n    },\n    {\n      accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n      id: 'lastName',\n      header: () => 'Last Name',\n      cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n    },\n    {\n      accessorKey: 'age',\n      header: () => 'Age',\n    },\n  ]\n\n  // 5. Create the table instance\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return data // a getter keeps the table in sync with the $state rune\n    },\n  })\n</script>\n\n<!-- 6. Render markup from the table instance APIs -->\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    {#each table.getHeaderGroups() as headerGroup (headerGroup.id)}\n      <tr>\n        {#each headerGroup.headers as header (header.id)}\n          <th>\n            {#if !header.isPlaceholder}\n              <FlexRender {header} />\n            {/if}\n          </th>\n        {/each}\n      </tr>\n    {/each}\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    {#each table.getRowModel().rows as row (row.id)}\n      <tr>\n        {#each row.getAllCells() as cell (cell.id)}\n          <td>\n            <FlexRender {cell} />\n          </td>\n        {/each}\n      </tr>\n    {/each}\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on `tableFeatures({...})` when you need them.\n- Passing `data` through a getter (`get data() { return data }`) lets the table track the `$state` rune, so reassigning `data` updates the table.\n- The `FlexRender` component renders the `header`, `cell`, and `footer` definitions from your columns. It handles plain values, Svelte components wrapped with `renderComponent`, and snippets wrapped with `renderSnippet` (see the [Basic Snippets example](./examples/basic-snippets)).\n\nSee the full [Basic createTable example](./examples/basic-create-table) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and `sortedRowModel` in `tableFeatures`, and wire a click handler into the header markup.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import {\n    createSortedRowModel,\n    createTable,\n    FlexRender,\n    rowSortingFeature,\n    sortFns,\n    tableFeatures,\n  } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\n  const features = tableFeatures({\n    rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n    sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n    sortFns,\n  })\n\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return data\n    },\n  })\n</script>\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    {#each table.getHeaderGroups() as headerGroup (headerGroup.id)}\n      <tr>\n        {#each headerGroup.headers as header (header.id)}\n          <th>\n            {#if !header.isPlaceholder}\n              <button\n                disabled={!header.column.getCanSort()}\n                onclick={header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()}\n              >\n                <FlexRender {header} />\n                {#if header.column.getIsSorted() === 'asc'}\n                  🔼\n                {:else if header.column.getIsSorted() === 'desc'}\n                  🔽\n                {/if}\n              </button>\n            {/if}\n          </th>\n        {/each}\n      </tr>\n    {/each}\n  </thead>\n  <!-- tbody unchanged from above -->\n</table>\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature and its row model together in `tableFeatures({...})`, then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows. See the [Sorting Guide](./guide/sorting.md) and the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms, and the Svelte adapter installs rune-based reactivity for you. You usually do not need to manage state yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When your app should own a state slice, or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md). It is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature guides.** Each feature has its own guide, such as [Column Filtering](./guide/column-filtering.md), [Pagination](./guide/pagination.md), [Row Selection](./guide/row-selection.md), and [Column Visibility](./guide/column-visibility.md).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { createAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })\n```\n\nSee the [Composable Tables Guide](./guide/composable-tables.md) for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Svelte examples](./examples/basic-create-table), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Quick Start\n---\n\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Svelte table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> This version of `@tanstack/svelte-table` only supports Svelte 5 or newer. For Svelte 3/4 support, use version 8 of `@tanstack/svelte-table`.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/svelte-table\n```\n\n## Your First Table\n\nThe component below is complete. Paste it into a Svelte 5 app and you will see a working table.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import {\n    createTable,\n    FlexRender,\n    tableFeatures,\n  } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n  import type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\n  // 1. Define the shape of your data\n  type Person = {\n    firstName: string\n    lastName: string\n    age: number\n  }\n\n  // 2. Store data with a $state rune for reactivity\n  let data = $state<Array<Person>>([\n    { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n    { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n    { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n  ])\n\n  // 3. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\n  const features = tableFeatures({})\n\n  // 4. Define your columns\n  const columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n    {\n      accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n      header: 'First Name',\n      cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n    },\n    {\n      accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n      id: 'lastName',\n      header: () => 'Last Name',\n      cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n    },\n    {\n      accessorKey: 'age',\n      header: () => 'Age',\n    },\n  ]\n\n  // 5. Create the table instance\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return data // a getter keeps the table in sync with the $state rune\n    },\n  })\n</script>\n\n<!-- 6. Render markup from the table instance APIs -->\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    {#each table.getHeaderGroups() as headerGroup (headerGroup.id)}\n      <tr>\n        {#each headerGroup.headers as header (header.id)}\n          <th>\n            {#if !header.isPlaceholder}\n              <FlexRender {header} />\n            {/if}\n          </th>\n        {/each}\n      </tr>\n    {/each}\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    {#each table.getRowModel().rows as row (row.id)}\n      <tr>\n        {#each row.getAllCells() as cell (cell.id)}\n          <td>\n            <FlexRender {cell} />\n          </td>\n        {/each}\n      </tr>\n    {/each}\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on `tableFeatures({...})` when you need them.\n- Passing `data` through a getter (`get data() { return data }`) lets the table track the `$state` rune, so reassigning `data` updates the table.\n- The `FlexRender` component renders the `header`, `cell`, and `footer` definitions from your columns. It handles plain values, Svelte components wrapped with `renderComponent`, and snippets wrapped with `renderSnippet` (see the [Basic Snippets example](./examples/basic-snippets)).\n\nSee the full [Basic createTable example](./examples/basic-create-table) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and `sortedRowModel` in `tableFeatures`, and wire a click handler into the header markup.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import {\n    createSortedRowModel,\n    createTable,\n    FlexRender,\n    rowSortingFeature,\n    sortFns,\n    tableFeatures,\n  } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\n  const features = tableFeatures({\n    rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n    sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n    sortFns,\n  })\n\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return data\n    },\n  })\n</script>\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    {#each table.getHeaderGroups() as headerGroup (headerGroup.id)}\n      <tr>\n        {#each headerGroup.headers as header (header.id)}\n          <th>\n            {#if !header.isPlaceholder}\n              <button\n                disabled={!header.column.getCanSort()}\n                onclick={header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()}\n              >\n                <FlexRender {header} />\n                {#if header.column.getIsSorted() === 'asc'}\n                  🔼\n                {:else if header.column.getIsSorted() === 'desc'}\n                  🔽\n                {/if}\n              </button>\n            {/if}\n          </th>\n        {/each}\n      </tr>\n    {/each}\n  </thead>\n  <!-- tbody unchanged from above -->\n</table>\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature and its row model together in `tableFeatures({...})`, then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows. See the [Sorting Guide](./guide/sorting.md) and the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms, and the Svelte adapter installs rune-based reactivity for you. You usually do not need to manage state yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When your app should own a state slice, or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md). It is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature guides.** Each feature has its own guide, such as [Column Filtering](./guide/column-filtering.md), [Pagination](./guide/pagination.md), [Row Selection](./guide/row-selection.md), and [Column Visibility](./guide/column-visibility.md).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { createAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })\n```\n\nSee the [Composable Tables Guide](./guide/composable-tables.md) for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Svelte examples](./examples/basic-create-table), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n"}
